# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The story is read here in the order Jesus tells it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another passage, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The opening and the request (15:11–12). “And he said, A certain man had two sons: And the younger of them said to his father, Father, give me the portion of goods that falleth to me. And he divided unto them his living.” The story begins with a man and two sons. The younger asks for a portion. The father divides “unto them” his living, and the text does not say what the elder received or did with it.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The journey and the waste (15:13). “And not many days after the younger son gathered all together, and took his journey into a far country, and there wasted his substance with riotous living.” The younger son gathers all together, takes his journey into a far country, and wastes his substance. The text gives no reason for the journey and names no country.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The want (15:14–16). “And when he had spent all, there arose a mighty famine in that land; and he began to be in want. And he went and joined himself to a citizen of that country; and he sent him into his fields to feed swine. And he would fain have filled his belly with the husks that the swine did eat: and no man gave unto him.” Four things are told in order: he has spent all; a famine arises; he joins himself to a citizen who sends him to feed swine; and “no man gave unto him.” The text does not name the citizen.'))
A(('p', '3.4 He came to himself (15:17–19). “And when he came to himself, he said, How many hired servants of my father’s have bread enough and to spare, and I perish with hunger! I will arise and go to my father, and will say unto him, Father, I have sinned against heaven, and before thee, And am no more worthy to be called thy son: make me as one of thy hired servants.” The text says what he says to himself, and it gives his words in three parts: the hired servants have bread enough; I will arise and go; and the words I will say. These are words he prepares. They are not yet spoken to the father.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The meeting (15:20). “And he arose, and came to his father. But when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him.” The son arises and comes. The father sees him “a great way off.” Four things the father does are told: had compassion, ran, fell on his neck, kissed him. The text does not say how long the father had watched or waited.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The son’s words (15:21). “And the son said unto him, Father, I have sinned against heaven, and in thy sight, and am no more worthy to be called thy son.” The son speaks to the father. The words of 15:21 follow the first two parts of the prepared words of 15:18–19. The last part, “make me as one of thy hired servants,” is not recorded here, and the text does not say why.'))
A(('p', '3.7 The father’s commands (15:22–24). “But the father said to his servants, Bring forth the best robe, and put it on him; and put a ring on his hand, and shoes on his feet: And bring hither the fatted calf, and kill it; and let us eat, and be merry: For this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found. And they began to be merry.” The father speaks to his servants, not to the son. He gives six commands: bring forth the best robe, put it on him, put a ring on his hand, shoes on his feet, bring the fatted calf, and kill it. He gives a reason, “For this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found.” The text ends the scene with “And they began to be merry.”'))
A(('p', '3.8 The elder son hears (15:25–27). “Now his elder son was in the field: and as he came and drew nigh to the house, he heard musick and dancing. And he called one of the servants, and asked what these things meant. And he said unto him, Thy brother is come; and thy father hath killed the fatted calf, because he hath received him safe and sound.” The elder son comes from the field and hears “musick and dancing.” He asks a servant. The servant gives two facts, “Thy brother is come” and “thy father hath killed the fatted calf,” and a reason, “because he hath received him safe and sound.”'))
A(('p', '3.9 The anger and the father’s coming out (15:28). “And he was angry, and would not go in: therefore came his father out, and intreated him.” The elder son is angry and will not go in. The father comes out and entreats him. The father has now gone out to the younger son (15:20) and to the elder (15:28).'))
A(('p', '3.10 The elder son’s answer (15:29–30). “And he answering said to his father, Lo, these many years do I serve thee, neither transgressed I at any time thy commandment: and yet thou never gavest me a kid, that I might make merry with my friends: But as soon as this thy son was come, which hath devoured thy living with harlots, thou hast killed for him the fatted calf.” The elder son gives three things: his service and his keeping of the commandment; that no kid was ever given him; and that the fatted calf was killed for “this thy son,” whom he charges with devouring the living “with harlots.” The text records his charge. It does not say whether the charge is a report of what happened.'))
A(('p', '3.11 The father’s answer (15:31–32). “And he said unto him, Son, thou art ever with me, and all that I have is thine. It was meet that we should make merry, and be glad: for this thy brother was dead, and is alive again; and was lost, and is found.” The father calls him “Son” and says two things of him: “thou art ever with me, and all that I have is thine.” He gives a reason for the feast: “It was meet that we should make merry, and be glad.” The father says “this thy brother,” where the elder son had said “this thy son.”'))
