A(('p', '3.12 What the story contains. The text names a man, two sons, a portion, a far country, riotous living, a famine, a citizen, swine, husks, hired servants, a robe, a ring, shoes, a fatted calf, musick and dancing, a field, a kid, and harlots. The action moves from a request, to a departure, to want, to a return, to a welcome and a feast, to an elder son who will not go in, to a father who comes out and speaks to him.'))
A(('p', '3.13 What the story leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say who the man is. Jesus does not say who the younger son or the elder son is. Jesus does not say what the far country, the famine, the citizen or the swine are. Jesus does not say what the robe, the ring, the shoes or the fatted calf are. Jesus does not say what the musick and dancing are, or who the servants are. The story ends with the father’s words at 15:32. It does not say whether the elder son went in, and it does not say what became of either son after the feast.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives no explanation. Luke records no explanation of this story and no request for one. The chapter ends at 15:32, and the next verse turns to a new saying, spoken “also unto his disciples” (16:1). This section therefore records no identification, because there is none to record.'))
A(('p', '4.2 The words in the story. The father says, “this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found” (15:24), and says it again to the elder son, “for this thy brother was dead, and is alive again; and was lost, and is found” (15:32). These are the father’s words inside the story. Luke does not present them as Jesus’ own statement of what the story means, and they are recorded here as words of the story and not as an identification.'))
A(('p', '4.3 The words of Luke 15:7 and 15:10. “Likewise, I say unto you, there is joy in the presence of the angels of God over one sinner that repenteth” (15:10) is a statement of Jesus attached to the coin, and the sheep has a like statement (15:7). They stand before this story. Luke 15:11 does not carry them forward, and no statement of that kind is attached to the two sons.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What Jesus does not identify. The story names many parts and leaves each without an explanation. Jesus does not identify the man. Jesus does not identify the younger son or the elder son. Jesus does not identify the portion, the far country, the famine or the citizen. Jesus does not identify the swine or the husks. Jesus does not identify the hired servants or the servants. Jesus does not identify the robe, the ring, the shoes or the fatted calf. Jesus does not identify the musick and dancing, the field, or the kid. Jesus does not identify the harlots.'))
