A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification for any of them.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives no identification, none is given here. A reader who says that the man is a certain person, that the sons are certain peoples, that the far country, the robe, the ring or the calf is a certain thing, or that the feast is a certain event is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.5 What the text does say. The text says that a man had two sons; that the younger took his portion, journeyed to a far country and wasted it; that a famine came and he fed swine; that he came to himself, rose and came to his father; that the father ran, embraced him and called for a robe, a ring, shoes and the fatted calf; that the elder son would not go in; and that the father came out and said, “Son, thou art ever with me, and all that I have is thine.” These statements are made by the story itself and are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'No explanation is given for this story, so this section states only the meaning that arises from the story itself. It assigns no identity to any part of it. The story does not begin with a comparison to the kingdom, and no such comparison is made here.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The story. Jesus tells of a man with two sons. The younger asks for his portion and wastes it, is in want, comes to himself and comes home, and is received with a run, a kiss and a feast. The elder is angry and will not go in, and the father comes out to him. The meaning is in what the story shows of the going out, the receiving and the staying outside.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the story shows.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A son who was away is received with joy.** “But when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran” (15:20). The welcome comes before the son’s speech is finished (15:21–22).',
    '**What was lost is spoken of as found.** The father says, “he was lost, and is found” (15:24). The words are the father’s, and they are said at the feast.',
    '**Grievance can keep a person outside a feast.** The elder son “was angry, and would not go in” (15:28). He is near the house and hears the music, and he stays outside.',
    '**The father goes out to both sons.** He runs to the younger (15:20) and comes out and entreats the elder (15:28).',
    '**The feast is called fitting.** The father says, “It was meet that we should make merry, and be glad” (15:32).',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The people Luke names in this scene are the publicans and sinners who drew near and the Pharisees and scribes who murmured (15:1–2). They had heard that the kingdom of God was being preached. They were meant to see a father who receives a returning son with joy and who also goes out to a son who will not come in. Luke does not say that the younger son is the publicans and sinners or that the elder son is the Pharisees and scribes, and Jesus does not identify either son.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the story. The force is in the last scene. The feast is under way, the lost son is home, and the story turns to the one who stayed. It ends with the father’s words to him and with no record of his answer. The story asks the hearer to see that a welcome can be received with joy and can be met with anger, and that the father speaks to the angry son as “Son.”'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the story supports, in short statements. A lost son is welcomed with joy. A father goes out to both of his sons. Grievance can keep a person from a feast that is under way. These statements arise from the story, and they do not say that the man, the sons, the far country, the robe, the ring, the calf or the feast represents anyone or anything. The story is not turned into a picture of how any person comes to God, and the younger son’s words and actions are not made a pattern of salvation.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The two sons is the third of three stories in Luke 15. It is kept apart here from the two stories before it, from the story of the same opening words in Matthew, and from the account that follows in Luke 16.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 15:4–10', '“What man of you, having an hundred sheep”; “Either what woman having ten pieces of silver”', 'The Lost Sheep and the Lost Coin. A man and his sheep, a woman and her coin, a search, a calling together of friends and neighbours, and a statement of Jesus about joy (15:7, 10). No sons, no far country and no statement of that kind is attached to the two sons. It stands in its own study.'],
    ['Matt 21:28–32', '“A certain man had two sons”', 'The Two Sons. The same opening words, and a different story: a command to work in a vineyard, an answer of “I will not” and “I go, sir,” and a question put to the hearers. The sons of that story are not the sons of Luke 15.'],
    ['Luke 16:1–13', '“a certain rich man, which had a steward”', 'The Shrewd Manager. Told “unto his disciples,” with a steward and a reckoning. It is a new setting and a new story, and nothing from it is read back into Luke 15.'],
    ['Luke 16:19–31', '“There was a certain rich man”', 'The Rich Man and Lazarus. A rich man and a beggar, in a new setting after the words to the Pharisees at 16:14–15. The “father” named in that account is not the father of Luke 15.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The two sons is told in Luke at the head of a chapter that begins with a complaint about receiving sinners (15:1–2), and it follows two stories told in the same place. The Two Sons of Matthew is told in the temple, after the question about authority (Matthew 21:23). A shared phrase such as “a certain man had two sons” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The story has a man, a younger son, an elder son, a citizen, servants and hired servants. The sheep and the coin have a man, a woman, friends and neighbours, and a shepherd’s and a woman’s search. The Two Sons of Matthew has a man, a first son and a second son, and a vineyard. No character is carried from one story into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The story moves from a request, to a departure, to want, to a return, to a feast, to an elder son who stays outside, and it stops at the father’s word. The sheep and the coin each move from a loss, to a search, to a finding, to a calling together of others. In the sheep and the coin, the one lost is sought. In this story the younger son is not sought out in the text. None of those movements is read into another.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Four details must not be carried across. First, “lost” and “found” are said of the sheep and the coin in their own stories and are said by the father in this one (15:24, 32). The words are the same, and the speakers and the stories are different. Second, the statements of Jesus at 15:7 and 15:10 are attached to the sheep and the coin, not to the two sons. Third, the “far country,” the robe, the ring and the fatted calf belong to this story only. Fourth, the two sons of Matthew 21:28–32 are not the two sons of Luke 15.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which Luke records that He was preaching the kingdom of God (4:43) and sent the Twelve to preach it (9:2). The two sons belongs to that ministry. Luke places the story among the publicans and sinners who drew near and the Pharisees and scribes who murmured (15:1–2), and it is read there.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The story was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The ministry in which Jesus told this story is the ministry in which the kingdom of God was being preached to those He spoke to. It is not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The man, the sons, the far country, the robe, the ring, the calf and the feast are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The story is not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. Coming to himself, arising, going, confessing and going in or staying out are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this story: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the story shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of welcome.** The father “ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him” (15:20). A reader learns to receive one who returns with joy and not with a demand.',
    '**A lesson of honesty.** The younger son says, “I have sinned against heaven, and in thy sight” (15:21). A reader learns to speak plainly of a wrong.',
    '**A lesson against grievance.** The elder son “was angry, and would not go in” (15:28). A reader learns that keeping a score of one’s own service can hold a person outside a shared joy.',
    '**A lesson of gentleness.** The father came out and “intreated him” (15:28). A reader learns to go out to the one who is angry and to speak to him as “Son.”',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The father’s feast is not turned into the Body’s hope, and the elder son’s grievance is not turned into a charge laid on the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this story.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'A man had two sons. The younger took his portion, journeyed to a far country, wasted it, and came to want in a famine. He came to himself, rose and came to his father, who saw him a great way off, ran, and kissed him, and called for a robe, a ring, shoes and the fatted calf. The elder son heard the music and would not go in, and the father came out and entreated him. The father’s last words are, “It was meet that we should make merry, and be glad: for this thy brother was dead, and is alive again; and was lost, and is found.” Jesus gives no explanation and names none of the parts, and the story ends without saying what the elder son did. The story shows a lost son received with joy, a father who goes out to both of his sons, and a grievance that keeps a man from a feast. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the man, the sons, the far country, the robe, the ring or the calf are, or that makes coming home a way of being saved, goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a request and a departure; waste and famine; a return; a father who runs; a robe, a ring and a feast; an elder son who will not go in; a father who comes out; the last words of the father.',
    '**The meaning:** a lost son is welcomed with joy; a father goes out to both sons; grievance can keep a person from a feast under way.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus; no identity for the man, the sons or any part of the story; nothing carried in from the sheep and the coin, the Two Sons of Matthew, or Luke 16.',
    '**The present lesson:** welcome, honesty, no grievance and gentleness, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9
A(('h1', S9))
A(('bul', [
    '**Between characters.** The man, the younger son, the elder son, the citizen, the servants and the hired servants are distinct, and Jesus names none of them as anyone.',
    '**Between actions.** The younger son asks, journeys, wastes, is in want, comes to himself, arises and speaks. The father divides, sees, runs, kisses, commands, comes out and entreats. The elder son hears, asks, is angry and answers.',
    '**Between words.** The younger son’s prepared words (15:18–19) are not the same as the words he speaks (15:21). The elder son’s “this thy son” (15:30) is answered by the father’s “this thy brother” (15:32).',
    '**Between settings.** This story stands in Luke 15 after the complaint. The Two Sons of Matthew stands in the temple. The account of Luke 16 is spoken “unto his disciples.”',
    '**Between this story and its neighbors.** The far country, the robe, the ring and the calf belong to this story only. The search and the statements of 15:7 and 15:10 belong to the sheep and the coin.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the man, the sons, the far country, the citizen, the servants, the robe, the ring, the calf or the feast. The Body of Christ is none of them.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from the sheep and the coin, the Two Sons of Matthew, or Luke 16.',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Coming to himself, arising, confessing and going in are actions in a story. They are not steps by which a person is saved.',
]))

