A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Lost Sheep and the Lost Coin, the Two Sons, the Shrewd Manager and the Rich Man and Lazarus are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. This story makes no use of their meanings, and they make none of this one.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. A man had two sons. The younger took his portion, went to a far country, wasted it, came to want and came home, and his father ran to him and called for a robe, a ring, shoes and the fatted calf. The elder son would not go in, and the father came out and entreated him. The father’s last words are that it was meet to make merry and be glad, for this his brother was dead, and is alive again; and was lost, and is found. Jesus gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The story shows a lost son welcomed with joy, a father who goes out to both sons, and a grievance that keeps a man from a feast.'))
